\section{Results}
\label{sec:results}

% [Main dual vulnerability driver results — to be written when P0 dominance analysis is finalized]

\subsection{Aggregation Rules Modulate the Diversity Channel}
\label{sec:aggregation}

The dual vulnerability driver effect varies substantially across aggregation methods. Table~\ref{tab:aggregation} reports regression results for each method across all 12 conditions.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Method} & \textbf{$\eta_{\max}$ sig} & \textbf{$K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ sig} & \textbf{$K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ $\beta$ dir.} & \textbf{$R^2$} \\
\midrule
Threshold       & 12/12 & 11/12 & 10+/1$-$  & 0.901 \\
Majority        & 11/12 & 8/12  & 7+/1$-$   & 0.376 \\
Weighted        & 11/12 & 11/12 & 11+/0$-$  & 0.357 \\
Bayesian        & 9/12  & 5/12  & 1+/4$-$   & 0.105 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{Aggregation method comparison (15 models, 455 panels, log-log canonical spec). Significance counts are FDR-corrected across 12 conditions. $R^2$ is the mean across conditions. Methods are ordered by $R^2$ to highlight the vulnerability gradient from threshold (most predictable) to Bayesian (least).}
\label{tab:aggregation}
\end{table}

Four patterns emerge. First, the aggregation methods form a clear vulnerability gradient: threshold voting ($R^2{=}0.901$) makes panel composition almost perfectly predictive of attack success, while Bayesian calibration ($R^2{=}0.105$) compresses the ASR distribution so that compositional factors explain little variance. Second, Bayesian calibration suppresses both vulnerability channels: $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ significance drops from 8/12 (majority) to 5/12, and the coefficient direction flips from predominantly positive (7+/1$-$) to predominantly negative (1+/4$-$), meaning diversity shifts from harmful to partially protective under proper reliability calibration. Bayesian also partially weakens $\eta_{\max}$ (11/12 $\to$ 9/12). Third, threshold voting amplifies both channels: $\eta_{\max}$ reaches 12/12 and $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ reaches 11/12, because unanimity requirements make panels maximally sensitive to both individual weakness and structural diversity. Fourth, weighted voting increases $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ significance (11/12, all positive) relative to majority voting, suggesting that accuracy weighting does not mitigate the diversity trap.

These results yield a prescriptive gradient. Bayesian calibration is the only aggregation method that suppresses the diversity channel and partially weakens the weakest-link channel, but it does not eliminate either. A two-pronged defense is needed: robustness-first panel selection to address $\eta_{\max}$, combined with Bayesian aggregation to mitigate $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ vulnerability. Neither intervention alone addresses both drivers.

\section{Threat Model and Problem Formulation}

A \emph{panel} $\mathcal{P} = \{J_1, \ldots, J_K\}$ consists of $K$ judges (with $K$ odd) that evaluate a candidate text via majority vote: the text is accepted if at least $t = \lceil K/2 \rceil$ judges approve it. Under an attack $a$, each judge $J_j$ is compromised with marginal failure probability $\eta_j(a) = \Pr(X_j^a = 1)$, where $X_j^a \in \{0,1\}$ denotes the failure indicator. We write $\eta_{\max} = \max_j \eta_j$ for the vulnerability of the weakest judge. The \emph{panel attack success rate} is $\mathrm{ASR}(\mathcal{P}, a) = \Pr\!\bigl(\sum_{j=1}^K X_j^a \geq t\bigr)$.

To quantify diversity, we define the \emph{effective independence} $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ directly from pairwise mutual information among judges:
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:keff}
K_{\mathrm{eff}} = \frac{1}{K}\sum_{i=1}^{K} \Bigl[1 + \sum_{j \neq i} \exp\!\bigl(-2 \cdot \mathrm{MI}(i,j)\bigr)\Bigr].
\end{equation}
This formulation computes effective independence from pairwise MI estimates without matrix decomposition, and is more stable for small panels ($K{=}3$). It ranges from $K_{\mathrm{eff}} \approx K$ when judges are nearly independent ($\mathrm{MI} \to 0$) to $K_{\mathrm{eff}} \approx 1$ when judges are highly correlated ($\mathrm{MI} \to \infty$). Under the strong data-processing inequality (SDPI), the joint vulnerability satisfies $\eta_{\mathrm{joint}} \leq \min(1,\; K_{\mathrm{eff}} \cdot \eta_{\max})$, suggesting that diversity should help---but this bound assumes non-adaptive faults.

\begin{definition}[Adaptive Adversary]
\label{def:adversary}
An \emph{adaptive adversary} has full knowledge of the panel composition $\mathcal{P}$, the per-judge vulnerability profiles $\{\eta_j(\cdot)\}_{j=1}^K$, and the inter-judge correlation structure. It selects the attack $a^* = \arg\max_{a \in \mathcal{A}} \mathrm{ASR}(\mathcal{P}, a)$ to maximize the panel ASR, yielding $\mathrm{ASR}^*(\mathcal{P}) = \sup_{a \in \mathcal{A}} \mathrm{ASR}(\mathcal{P}, a)$.
\end{definition}

This departs from Byzantine fault-tolerance (BFT) models~\cite{lamport1982byzantine,castro1999practical}, which tolerate arbitrary (including malicious) faults provided the number of faulty nodes satisfies $f < n/3$, but do not model adversaries that strategically target system structure. An adaptive adversary instead concentrates attack effort on the weakest link~\cite{schneier2000secrets}, rendering diversity-based defenses less effective than the SDPI bound implies.

The distinction reveals a \emph{reversal mechanism}: under random faults, independent judges fail via uncorrelated events, and majority voting aggregates away individual errors---higher $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ reduces joint failure probability. Under adaptive adversaries, however, independent judges present a \emph{wider attack surface}: the adversary can exploit judge-specific vulnerabilities across a broader space of attacks, because functionally diverse judges respond to different manipulation strategies. Diversity ceases to provide mutual protection and instead expands the set of viable attack vectors.

\begin{theorem}[Weakest-Link Monotonicity]
\label{thm:monotonicity}
Let $\mathcal{P}$ and $\mathcal{P}'$ be two panels sharing $K{-}1$ judges and differing only in the $K$-th member, where $J_K'$ is pointwise more vulnerable than $J_K$ (i.e., $\Pr(X_{K'}^a {=} 1 \mid \mathbf{X}_{-K}^a {=} \mathbf{x}_{-K}) \geq \Pr(X_K^a {=} 1 \mid \mathbf{X}_{-K}^a {=} \mathbf{x}_{-K})$ for all attacks $a$ and realizations $\mathbf{x}_{-K}$). Then for any adversary, including an adaptive one: $\mathrm{ASR}^*(\mathcal{P}') \geq \mathrm{ASR}^*(\mathcal{P})$.
\end{theorem}

\noindent Proof in Appendix~\ref{app:proof}. Intuitively, the replaced judge is \emph{pivotal} exactly when $t{-}1$ of the remaining judges have already failed; in such configurations, a more vulnerable replacement can only increase the probability of tipping the majority, never decrease it. The result holds under arbitrary inter-judge correlations and any attack space $\mathcal{A}$.

The theorem establishes that $\eta_{\max}$ is monotonically linked to panel vulnerability, but is silent on the role of diversity. To disentangle the two drivers, we fit a regression $\log(\mathrm{ASR}) \sim \beta_1 \log(\eta_{\max}) + \beta_2 \log(K_{\mathrm{eff}})$ across panels. The theorem predicts $\beta_1 > 0$; the SDPI bound under non-adaptive faults would suggest $\beta_2 \leq 0$; but the reversal mechanism predicts $\beta_2 > 0$ when attacks exploit judge-specific vulnerabilities, and $\beta_2 < 0$ when attacks exploit shared cognitive biases that diverse panels can dilute---a distinction our experiments in \S\ref{sec:experiments} test across 12 conditions spanning two benchmarks and six attack types, using dominance analysis to isolate each driver's independent contribution.

